# Adapters

Everything under `internal/adapter/` speaks domain types and domain errors.
Three shapes:

| Adapter | Ships with | Shape |
|---|---|---|
| `postgres/` | `gostack new --postgres` | `pkg/postgres` pool, sqlc queries, `MapError` |
| `redis/` | `gostack new --redis` | `pkg/redis` client, key prefix, TTLs |
| any other name | `gostack g adapter <name>` | stateful, in-process, no `pkg/` layer |

## `g adapter` — the stateful shape

`gostack g adapter inmemory` scaffolds a root `Adapter` behind a `sync.Mutex`,
a `wire.Set`, and `example_storage.go` demonstrating the pattern. Extend it as
far as you need — TTL, eviction, counters — the rules below are what keep it
an adapter rather than a stray map:

- **One storage = one file** `<name>_storage.go`: the struct holding its
  state plus the `*Adapter` methods that operate on it.
- **State is only touched under `a.mu`.** Lock per method; never hold the
  lock across anything that can block.
- **Map outcomes to domain errors.** A miss is `domain.ErrNotFound`, a
  duplicate is `domain.ErrAlreadyExists` — the same contract postgres's
  `MapError` gives, so a use case cannot tell which store it is talking to
  and a later swap to redis or postgres is local to this package.
- **Take `context.Context` even when unused** — same swap argument.
- **Restart wipes everything.** Right for caches, rate limiters, counters,
  dev without docker, stubs before a schema exists. Anything a user would
  notice losing belongs in Postgres.

TTL is a field, not a framework: store `expiresAt time.Time` next to the
value and treat an expired entry as a miss.

## Wire

The generated `Set` is deliberately absent from `cmd/wire.go`: wire rejects a
provider set nothing consumes. Add `<pkg>.Set` to `wire.Build` when the first
use case takes `*<pkg>.Adapter` in its constructor — the same rule a bare
`g uc` follows.
